'''A10 Networks, Inc.''' is an American public company specializing in the manufacturing of [[application delivery controller]]s (software and hardware). Founded in 2004 by Lee Chen,<ref>{{Cite newsletter | title = No Fear | accessdate = 2010-01-06 | url = http://www.a10networks.com/news/files/summer07-sjsu.pdf | work = San Jose State University Engineering Newsletter | publisher = San Jose State University Engineering | date = 2007-01-21 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20101204132205/http://www.a10networks.com/news/files/summer07-sjsu.pdf | archive-date = 2010-12-04 | url-status = dead }}</ref> co-founder of [[Foundry Networks]], A10 originally serviced just the [[identity management]] market with its line of ID Series products.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Rogers|first=James |title = A10 Networks | accessdate = 2009-02-20 | url = http://www.byteandswitch.com/document.asp?doc_id=84599 | work = Byte and Switch | publisher = United Business Media Limited | date = 2005-11-21 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20070608200353/http://www.byteandswitch.com/document.asp?doc_id=84599 | archive-date = 2007-06-08 | url-status = dead }}</ref> In early 2007, they added [[bandwidth management]] appliances (EX Series).<ref name=eos>{{cite web |title=End of Sales - EX Series |url-access=subscription|url=https://www.a10networks.com/support/end-of-sales/|website=A10 Networks}}</ref> The company had its initial public offering on March 21, 2014, raising $187.5 million.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Rubin |first1=Ben Fox |title=A10 Networks Shares Volatile in First Day of Trading |url=https://www.wsj.com/articles/SB10001424052702304256404579453302038467122 |website=Wall Street Journal |date=21 March 2014}}</ref>

==History==
In mid-2007, A10 Networks launched its AX Series of [[application delivery controller]]s/[[Load balancing (computing)|load balancing]] appliances.<ref name=eos/>

On May 21, 2013, A10 resolved its question of responsibility for intellectual property infringement and unfair competition practices by reaching a settlement with [[Brocade Communications Systems]].<ref>{{Cite press release |title = A10 Networks and Brocade Reach Settlement of Legal Disputes |date= May 21, 2013 |work = A10 Networks | publisher = A10 Networks |accessdate = August 28, 2013 |url = http://www.a10networks.com/news/2013/20130520-A10_Networks_and_Brocade_Reach_Settlement_of_Legal_Disputes.php }}</ref> Brocade had earlier been awarded $112 million in 2012.<ref>{{Cite news |title = Brocade awarded $112M verdict in dispute with A10 |date= August 7, 2012 |author= John Ribeiro |work= Computer World |accessdate = July 12, 2013 |url = http://www.computerworld.com/s/article/9230038/Brocade_awarded_112M_verdict_in_dispute_with_A10 }}</ref>

In May 2013, A10 launched its A10 Thunder Series platforms of hardware and software application delivery controllers (ADCs).<ref>{{Cite press release | title = A10 Unveils Thunder Series Unified Application Service Gateways  | accessdate = 2013-05-01 | url = http://www.a10networks.com/news/2013/20130501-A10_Networks_Unveils_Thunder_Series_Unified_Application_Service_Gateways.php | work = A10 Networks | publisher = A10 Networks | date = 2013-05-01 }}</ref> 

A10 Networks released the Harmony design of the Thunder Series ADC in 2015.<ref name="harmony2015">{{cite web|title=A10 Goes Beyond the Command Line |last1=Wagner |first1=Mitch |url=https://www.lightreading.com/spit-(service-provider-it)/cloud-enablement/a10-goes-beyond-the-command-line/d/d-id/713117 |work=Light Reading |date= January 20, 2015 |accessdate=October 9, 2019}}</ref>

Also in 2015, A10 Networks upgraded the Advanced Core Operating System (ACOS). The update allowed 100 percent of software capabilities to be addressed by [[Application programming interface|APIs]], whereas the previous ACOS could only address 40 percent through APIs.<ref name="harmony2015"/>

In 2016, A10 acquired the cloud-native ADC company Appcito.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.crn.com/news/networking/300081427/a10-networks-acquires-appcito-to-become-first-cloud-native-adc-company.htm|title=A10 Networks Acquires Appcito To Become 'First Cloud-Native' ADC Company|last=Haranas|first=Mark|date=2016-07-26|work=CRN|access-date=2018-02-20|language=en}}</ref>

In 2021, according to the companies [[Form 10-K|annual report]], A10 Networks closed its India and China offices and reduce its headcount.{{r|10K}}{{rp|58}}

